        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137466,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 59,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most syringomas arise in the lower eyelids and periorbital area {8599451}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137467,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 59,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syringoma presents as skin-coloured or slightly yellow smooth papules. Multiple syringomas may resemble milia cysts. When the lesions are pigmented, they may simulate urticaria pigmentosa or confluent and reticulated papillomatosis of Gougerot&ndash;Carteaud.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137468,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 59,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syringomas most commonly affect the lower eyelids of middle-aged to older women, but the eruptive pattern is most common in young women {11683289}. Clear cell syringoma is more common in people with diabetes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137469,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 59,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syringomas are common in patients with Down syndrome {7971561}, and they have also been described in patients with hyperthyroidism, Ehlers&ndash;Danlos syndromes, Marfan syndrome, and Nicolau&ndash;Balus syndrome {7262384}. In familial forms, multiple syringomas affect several family members.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137470,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 59,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syringomas resemble the ductal portion of the eccrine gland.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137471,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 59,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137472,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 59,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syringomas are well-circumscribed lesions typically affecting the upper half of the dermis; occasionally they can extend into the deep dermis or even the subcutis. Syringoma has two components: epithelial cells forming ducts, cords, tubules, and cysts, and a homogeneous and sclerotic stroma. The lumina of the ductal and tubular structures are lined by a double layer of cuboidal cells, sometimes containing a granular basophilic material. The ducts often show characteristic extensions resembling a tadpole tail. Solid collections of epithelial cells are also observed, often containing multiple confluent cytoplasmic vacuoles. Small cysts are often present, filled with basket-weave, basophilic, and orthokeratotic keratin and lined by squamous epithelium. The stroma consists of thick bundles of compact and sclerotic collagen. In eruptive syringoma, the stroma is less prominent.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>In superficial biopsies, the differential diagnosis includes microcystic adnexal carcinoma, sclerosing/morphoeic basal cell carcinoma, and desmoplastic trichoepithelioma&nbsp;/ columnar trichoblastoma, from which distinction may be impossible without complete removal and clinicopathological correlation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
